fact WorkItem(id: "WI-804", description: some(value: "Restore NAME-KEYED width subtyping for data tuples: (A: TA, B: TB, C: TC) <: (A: TA, C: TC) must hold, with a component dropped from ANYWHERE, not only the end. WI-788 refused the middle drop, and the argument it gave was that dropping B renumbers C from _3 to _2 and so changes the type. That is an argument about IDENTITY applied to <:, which is a DIFFERENT RELATION -- the consumer of an (a, c)-typed value asks for .a and .c, and an (a, b, c) value answers both wherever the components sit. Order belongs to identity; it has no part in <:. THE NARROWING ALSO DID NOT BUY SAFETY, measured both ways: the PREFIX drop WI-788 ALLOWED breaks a destructuring reader exactly as the middle drop would (both change the binder COUNT, so the match fails and raises), so the rule refused a correct program while admitting an equally broken one -- anti-correlated with what actually breaks. THE PRE-WI-788 SPEC HAD THIS RIGHT and WI-788 rewrote it: \"Permutation and width are subtyping rules for data tuples whose components are read by NAME ... (A data tuple read by DESTRUCTURING is also positional; aligning such a reader by name is a known open defect, tracked as WI-788).\" The relation was correct and the READER was named as the defect. DELIVERED HERE: align_named_tuple_fields' data arm is name-keyed and order-PRESERVING (a cursor scan, so a dropped component may come from anywhere but a permutation is still refused); the ORDER requirement is recorded in code and spec as an explicit INTERIM, not as the rule. WHY THE INTERIM IS SOUND TO HOLD: a destructuring binder list reads by SLOT and COUNT, so it is unsound under any <: step, but the failure modes differ -- WIDTH changes the count and fails LOUDLY, while PERMUTATION keeps the count and swaps the VALUES, silently binding a component the typer typed from a different field (an operation declared -> Int64 returning a String on a clean load). So width is admitted now and order is held until the reader binds by label, which is the real WI-788 and is tracked separately. Unit is excluded from width: () is not \"the record with no fields\" but the UNIT sort, which spec 4.5 gives exactly ONE value, so a wider tuple does not conform to it. DOCS: kernel-language.md 4.5 (width paragraph plus the interim note), the Arrow-types paragraph, proposal 004 rule 4a, and CLAUDE.md all state that identity and <: are different relations and that carrying the order rule across from one to the other is the mistake to avoid. ACCEPTANCE: width_middle_drop_conforms drives (a: 3, b: \"ess\", c: 10) into a (a: Int64, c: Int64) parameter and evaluates; the permutation tests stay refused; full workspace green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-788"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-21T14:18:41Z"))
